### What happens when a constructor throws?

The new expression never returns. The variable stays null, and the partially-constructed object is unreachable.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ConstructorExceptionDemo {

    static class TradeProcessor {
        TradeProcessor(String config) {
            System.out.println("Step 1: config loaded");
            if (config.equals("BAD")) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid config: " + config);
            }
            System.out.println("Step 2: processor ready");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TradeProcessor processor = null;
        try {
            processor = new TradeProcessor("BAD");
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        System.out.println("processor is: " + processor);
        // processor is null. The new expression never returned.
    }
}
```

```text
Step 1: config loaded
Caught: Invalid config: BAD
processor is: null
```

### How do resource leaks happen in constructors?

If the constructor opens resources and then throws, those resources are allocated but nobody has a reference to close them.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ConstructorLeakDemo {

    static class LeakyProcessor {
        private final String connection;
        private final String fileHandle;

        LeakyProcessor() {
            this.connection = "DB-Connection-Open";
            System.out.println("Opened: " + connection);

            this.fileHandle = "File-Handle-Open";
            System.out.println("Opened: " + fileHandle);

            // This line throws AFTER both resources are opened
            throw new RuntimeException("Schema validation failed");
            // connection and fileHandle are LEAKED.
            // Nobody has a reference to close them.
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            new LeakyProcessor();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Resources are leaked. No reference to close them.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Opened: DB-Connection-Open
Opened: File-Handle-Open
Caught: Schema validation failed
Resources are leaked. No reference to close them.
```

### How do you fix resource leaks in constructors?

Use try-catch with temporary variables to clean up on failure.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class SafeConstructorDemo {

    static class SafeProcessor {
        private final String connection;
        private final String fileHandle;

        SafeProcessor() {
            String tempConn = null;
            String tempFile = null;
            try {
                tempConn = "DB-Connection";
                System.out.println("Opened: " + tempConn);

                tempFile = "File-Handle";
                System.out.println("Opened: " + tempFile);

                // Simulate failure
                throw new RuntimeException("Validation failed");

            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                // Clean up everything opened so far
                if (tempFile != null) System.out.println("Closed: " + tempFile);
                if (tempConn != null) System.out.println("Closed: " + tempConn);
                throw e;  // Rethrow after cleanup
            }
            // This line only reached on success:
            // this.connection = tempConn;
            // this.fileHandle = tempFile;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            new SafeProcessor();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Resources cleaned up before rethrowing.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Opened: DB-Connection
Opened: File-Handle
Closed: File-Handle
Closed: DB-Connection
Caught: Validation failed
Resources cleaned up before rethrowing.
```

### What is the static factory method pattern?

A static factory manages the resource lifecycle outside the constructor, ensuring cleanup on failure.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class FactoryPatternDemo {

    static class TradeProcessor implements AutoCloseable {
        private final String connection;

        private TradeProcessor(String connection) {
            this.connection = connection;
        }

        static TradeProcessor create(String config) {
            String conn = "DB-Conn-" + config;
            System.out.println("Opened: " + conn);
            try {
                validate(config);
                return new TradeProcessor(conn);
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                System.out.println("Closed: " + conn);  // Cleanup on failure
                throw e;
            }
        }

        private static void validate(String config) {
            if (config.equals("BAD")) throw new RuntimeException("Invalid config");
        }

        @Override public void close() {
            System.out.println("Closed: " + connection);
        }

        public static void main(String[] args) {
            try {
                TradeProcessor p = TradeProcessor.create("BAD");
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Opened: DB-Conn-BAD
Closed: DB-Conn-BAD
Caught: Invalid config
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Finalizers and Cleaners.** A partially-constructed object whose constructor threw will NOT have its finalizer called (pre-Java 9) and should not rely on Cleaner (Java 9+) for resource cleanup.
- **Builder pattern.** The Builder validates everything in build() before constructing the object, preventing partial construction entirely.
- **Spring beans.** If a Spring @Service constructor throws, the ApplicationContext fails to start. Spring handles cleanup of already-constructed beans.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can the caller access a partially-constructed object? (No, the new expression never returns.)
- What happens to the partially-constructed object? (It is unreachable and GC'd.)
- How do you prevent resource leaks in constructors? (try-catch with cleanup, or static factory.)
- Is the object's finalizer called if the constructor throws? (No.)
